# =============================================================================
# .dockerignore — files/dirs NOT sent to the Docker build context.
#
# Why this matters: Docker sends EVERYTHING in the build context (the
# directory containing the Dockerfile) to the Docker daemon as a tarball
# before it starts the build. If your repo has 2GB of build artifacts, .git
# history, and node_modules, every `docker build` ships 2GB to the daemon
# even though none of it ends up in the image. .dockerignore excludes those
# paths from the context.
#
# This list is intentionally conservative — we exclude only things that are
# (a) large, (b) regenerated by the build, or (c) environment-specific.
# =============================================================================

# ── Build artifacts (regenerated during the docker build) ──
**/bin/
**/obj/
**/out/
**/publish/

# ── IDE / editor state (env-specific, never useful in an image) ──
.vs/
.vscode/
.idea/
*.user
*.suo
*.userprefs

# ── VCS ──
.git/
.gitignore
.gitattributes

# ── Docker tooling itself (don't recursively include) ──
Dockerfile
*.dockerfile
.dockerignore
docker-compose*.yml

# ── Local runtime state (would be wrong inside the image) ──
*.log
*.md
logs/

# ── Secrets (DEFENSIVE — these shouldn't be in the build context at all,
#    but if they are, refuse to bake them into the image. The .env file
#    here is for local-dev use; the docker-compose.yml in this repo bakes
#    real passwords directly, so .env is unused. Excluded for safety.)
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# ── OS junk ──
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# ── Documentation (large, not needed at runtime) ──
DEPLOYMENT.md
README-FIRST.md
